<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<configuration>

  <!--
    One source, and a <clear /> so that it is the ONLY one.

    Without the clear, a restore resolves against whatever sources happen to be configured on the
    machine doing it, which for most developers includes at least one private feed belonging to an
    employer. Two things go wrong with that, and the second is worse than the first.

    The obvious one: a package that resolves from a private feed builds for the author and for
    nobody else, and everyone else gets a package-not-found that names no reason.

    The one that matters here: this repository's central claim, asserted by the `dependencies` CI
    job and written down in docs/LICENSING.md, is that the shipped libraries depend on nothing
    outside the BCL. A restore that can reach a feed nobody else can audit undermines that claim
    even when it happens to pull nothing, because the guarantee stops being checkable.

    Everything this repository consumes — xunit, SkiaSharp, Silk.NET, the test SDK, all of it in
    tests only — is on nuget.org. If that ever stops being true, the honest fix is to add the feed
    here, in the open, with a reason next to it.
  -->
  <packageSources>
    <clear />
    <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" />
  </packageSources>

</configuration>
